/-!
# The correspondence check, run by the kernel

The `agda2lean` pipeline's last stage compares a *generated Agda-shaped facade*
with a *hand-written native Lean implementation* and issues a receipt only if
they agree under the fixture's policy — `statement = "lean-defeq"`,
`axioms = "target-subset"`, `allowed-lean-axioms = []`.

This project already has both sides of that comparison for `JFixedPoint.agda`,
so the comparison can simply be run:

* the facade is `AgdaVendor/JFixedPoint.lean`, which is the upstream Agda module
  read through this project's Agda front end (`RequestProject/Agda/Syntax.lean`),
  with the original hyphenated names preserved by Lean's `«…»` escaping — the
  same escaping convention the upstream emitter uses;
* the native implementation is `RequestProject/Mirror/JFixedPoint.lean`.

§1 is upstream's own gate `test/lean/JFixedPointCheck.lean`, which type-checks
here verbatim against the front end's output.  §2 discharges the `lean-defeq`
policy: every declaration of the facade is definitionally the corresponding
native one, so each check below is closed by `rfl`.  §3 discharges the
`allowed-lean-axioms = []` policy by printing the axiom closure of each mirrored
theorem and asserting that it is empty; `#guard_msgs` turns a change in that
closure into a build failure.
-/

